WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.400
NITIN MOTGI: Hello everyone,
and welcome to Building Data

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.210
Lake on GCP Talk.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.440
My name is Nitin Motgi.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.160
I'm a group product manager
in the data analytics group.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.870
So today we we've
only be talking

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.080
about how you build data
lakes, but let us first

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.170
understand what a data lake is.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.170
Data lake is a centralized
and a secure data platform

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.950
for ingesting, storing,
processing, and analyzing

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.350
all your data structured,
semi-structured,

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.080
and unstructured data at scale.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.270
When we work with
our customers we

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.445
find out that the traditional
approach to building data

00:00:39.445 --> 00:00:41.820
lakes, when you bring data
from various different sources

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.110
and land them into one single
central store, has not worked,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.270
and has not provided
good results.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.670
And they have also
told us that they

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.240
would like to have more
use case-driven data lakes.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.950
So the traditional
approach basically

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.520
ended up creating,
instead of lakes,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.430
it ended up creating swamps.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:04.410
And too much of
operational cost was

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.110
incurred during the
maintaining of those lakes.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.330
But when you look at what
those data lakes are,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.550
and what is the value
proposition of having a data

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.800
lake, there are many.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.440
One of them, to begin
with, is cost savings.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.760
You save a lot of
operational and capital cost

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.860
in essentially doing all of
these transformations outside

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.360
of your traditional
data warehouse.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.150
It also increases the
quality of the data.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.980
As the data actually gets
processed in the lake,

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.550
the quality of the data as
it progresses to the lake

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.450
increases dramatically.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.050
Now, if you build
a good data lake,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.540
it has to have a fast
discovery mechanism which

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.940
will allow you to
identify all kinds of data

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.210
that exists within the lake.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.000
And then, when you think about
what actually this data lake is

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.500
going to provide
for you, it's going

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.060
to reduce the time
from analytics.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.480
It's going to basically make
it easier for doing analytics

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.070
that you haven't been
able to do it before.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.990
And with having better
analytic explanations,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.390
you're able to get
to market faster.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.520
Now, there are very
different components

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.139
that are involved
in building a lake.

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:12.870
Whether it is you're
doing a data discovery,

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.360
you're organizing your data,
you're doing transformations,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.118
you're doing
optimizations, there

00:02:17.118 --> 00:02:18.660
are various different
components that

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.360
have to be included together
to build the best data lake.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.270
Now, let's see what are some of
the challenges that are there

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.150
in building the idea of
a data lake solution.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.090
Data lakes are complex
and time consuming,

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:33.860
The process of building
them and managing

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.160
them is extremely difficult.
And the reasons for these things

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.790
to be difficult
is because you are

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.640
doing a lot of various different
operations within the lake.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.920
First of them that
you begin with

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.900
is importing and ingesting data.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.220
It's a very laborious process.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.720
You have to first identify all
of the various different data

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.050
sources that exist,
within your organization,

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.900
which could be of various
different formats,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.910
and various different
data sources,

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.000
that you would have to bring
all that data together using

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.510
some form of an injection tool.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.940
Now, once you land
the data in the lake,

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.230
you have to organize it.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.030
And if you can't
organize it very well,

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.890
it just turns out into a swamp.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.070
So you have to spend
huge amounts of time

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.970
organizing the data in the lake.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.370
So it is basically,
you categorize the data

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.403
in various segments.

00:03:19.403 --> 00:03:21.070
You basically store
them, separate them,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.435
segregate them, and also assign
different security policies

00:03:24.435 --> 00:03:26.070
for them.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.690
The next thing that
is particularly

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.720
important in the lake
is data transformations.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.420
The data transformation
provides the standardized way

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.580
of transforming the
data from a raw form

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.700
to a standardized form, and
then actually preparing them

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.070
for analytics.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.940
Now combining all of
these things together,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.360
the most important
part that will

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.860
bringing all of the
[INAUDIBLE] of a data lake

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.315
is the discovery.

00:03:51.315 --> 00:03:54.100
If there exists no good
discovery mechanism

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.820
in a lake, the data
itself is lost.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:00.420
And it does incur dark data,
and more such dark data actually

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.800
leads to creating a swamp,
rather than a lake that you

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.300
would like to have.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.740
And the data quality
aspect of a data lake

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.760
is critically important
for a few reasons.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.020
One, if you look
at traditionally

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.180
why data lakes were
built, data lakes

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.785
were originally built to
offer a lot of the work that

00:04:17.785 --> 00:04:19.410
was happening within
the data warehouse

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.470
so that they could use the CPU
cycles of the data warehouse

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.190
to run the queries.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.280
So when you bring
all of that data

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.220
in, and combine that with all
of the structured, unstructured,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.840
and semi-structured
data put together,

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.430
you have to have a very
cohesive strategy for ensuring

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.500
the quality of the
data as it progresses

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.730
through the data lake towards
the data warehouse or data

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:41.800
model.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.000
So there has to be tools that
have to be integrated in order

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.000
to have guaranteed data
quality for the data moving

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.590
through the data lake.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.870
The last, but not the
least, you want the lake,

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.600
but you don't want
a lake that is not

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.982
accessible from various
different groups.

00:04:56.982 --> 00:04:58.440
Whether they are
open-source tools,

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.040
whether they are proprietary
tools, it doesn't matter.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.040
The lake has to be accessible
to all kinds of tools that

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.030
exist within the ecosystem.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.920
So interoperability
plays a very vital role

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.260
in ensuring that your data
lakes are not isolated,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.740
but they're integrated
into all of the ecosystems.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.090
So lakes can be simple.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.910
There are three primary
pillars on which the lakes can

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:26.570
be built. One, ability to
quickly build the lakes, which

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:28.790
is essentially,
involving populating data

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.550
from various different
sources into the lake.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.410
Once you bring the
data into the lake,

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.310
you'll need to be able
to catalog that data.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:37.980
You need to be able
to discover that.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:41.310
You need to make it easy to
find all of the data that exists

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.790
in the lake, So you would have
to have some kind of an ability

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:48.390
to crawl all of that metadata
that exists within the lake,

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:52.160
and store that into
a central repository.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.680
Once the data is
cataloged, it then

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.860
has to be indexed so that it's
easily discoverable to a search

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:58.740
mechanism.

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.050
And there are many
aspects of it.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.082
The metadata can include a lot
of different types of metadata

00:06:06.082 --> 00:06:07.040
that you can integrate.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.510
One is the file format.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:12.590
The other would be various
different metadata associated

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.600
with it, the size of it, the
time, the date it was updated.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.770
So all the different metadata
associated with the data

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:22.820
can be aggregated
with this category.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.220
The next is standardize
and cleansing

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.650
the data So as the
data lands in the lake,

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.550
the data has to be cleansed,
it has to be turned into--

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.530
it has to be
standardized, and it

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:35.330
has to be canonicalize
to conform

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.580
to a standard format
that is appropriate

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:38.600
for the organization.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.570
Things like SSM, things
like phone numbers, things

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:42.750
like zip codes--

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:46.220
all these have to be processed
in a standard fashion.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.520
And they have to be
made easy to do that.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.308
So all these transformations
takes a large amount of time.

00:06:51.308 --> 00:06:52.850
So these things have
to be simplified

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:54.110
as part of the process.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.720
So you wouldn't want to have
some kind of a code-free tool

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.900
that will allow you to do that.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:03.200
Now that you have brought
the data into the lake,

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.020
the first thing you want
to do is secure that data.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.420
You want to be able to
define access control.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.690
And you also want to
normally just do it

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.600
on the data, but also the
applications that are going

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:15.890
to be accessing that data.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:17.780
It's critically important.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.530
Data access control
is one aspect of it,

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:23.540
but the applications that are
going to be accessing the data

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:27.580
also have to be controlled in
terms of who, basically, has

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.080
the permissions to run the jobs
that are inevitability going

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.510
to be accessing that data.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.000
Now, in the lake, there
are so many different jobs

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.350
that are run--

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.210
jobs that are
ingesting the data,

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.040
jobs that are
standardizing it, jobs

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.620
that are transforming it and
preparing it for analytics,

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.660
preparing it for warehouses.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.045
Now all of this,
in an enterprise,

00:07:47.045 --> 00:07:49.930
are based on a scheduled
time window during which

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.400
these jobs have to be executed.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.660
So in order to get a better
insight into those metrics,

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.597
you would have to have
ability to understand

00:07:59.597 --> 00:08:01.430
all of the different
metrics that are there.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:03.440
You would have to
have access to them,

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.320
as well as being able to look
at the end-to-end assembly

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.390
of the jobs that are
all needed in the lake.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.960
Some kind of a dashboard, like
a data operations dashboard,

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.590
is required to enable these.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.390
Now because the lake is
a central place where

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:22.260
critical data exists,
and, as you all know,

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:25.860
of all the data that is in
the lake, 20% of the data

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.750
is actually very critical.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.120
And it's being used by
a lot of different parts

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:31.870
of the organization.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:35.190
So, in order to ensure
good security mechanism

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.020
and compliance
mechanism, there needs

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.440
to exist error logs that
an administrator can

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.370
use to see and improve the
access control as well as

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.090
the security for the data lake.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.260
Now once you have
ingested the data

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.060
and you are provided
the standardization

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.600
tools to essentially
transform the data,

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.220
and you have secured
it, the next thing

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.680
which is critically
important for the lake,

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.240
is to make the lake accessible.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:06.720
Like the lake,
today, if it is not

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.450
accessible to the world,
or the pools outside,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.180
people will not find
the lake usable.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.430
So first of all,
the lake should be

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.410
able to expose all
of the data that it

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:17.850
has in various different tools.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:21.240
Whether you're using Spark,
whether you are using Hive,

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.390
whether you are using PRESTO,
or you are using BigQuery,

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:25.340
it doesn't matter.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.270
The data that sits
in the lake has

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.510
to be immediately
accessible to proprietary,

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:33.670
as well as open source tools.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.850
In addition to that, the data
has to be smartly partitioned.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:39.520
Because what ends
up happening is,

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.530
as there are various
different tools

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:45.300
and various different needs from
an optimization perspective,

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.320
the data has to be
partitioned in such a way

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:51.430
that it is integrate
seamlessly with all

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:52.660
of these different tools.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.900
And that is, from an
analytics improvement,

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.140
there needs to be handling of
partitioning of the data which

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:02.160
actually is critical.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.170
Then, at the end
of it, you would

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.840
have to have standard
ways of moving data

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.090
from various different areas
within-- we can call them

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.780
zones-- various different
zones within the lake

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:14.880
that you define.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.030
There needs to be a standardized
way of my moving that data.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.410
Because, in some
cases, you might

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.190
want to move the data
from the defined zone

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:26.460
directly into your
data warehouse.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.160
So you're having that
ability which is [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.670
and allowing you to connect
with the world outside

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:36.480
is extremely critical for
making the lake successful.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.120
With these three
simple pillars, you

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.790
can build an end-to-end lake.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:46.790
Now, there are many use
cases that can use this lake.

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:49.170
And let's briefly look
at the kinds of use cases

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.300
that we have seen
with our customers,

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.810
and how they're actually
using all of the GCP products

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:57.580
and bringing them
together in the use cases.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.310
Let's start with
a simple use case.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.440
First of all, data
warehouse staging.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.810
And this was the primary purpose
for which data lakes were

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:07.080
built. They wanted
to offload all

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:11.700
of the additional ELT,
the transformation that

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:12.998
happens in the warehouse.

00:11:12.998 --> 00:11:14.540
They wanted to
offload it in the lake

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.750
so that it provides a
lot of cost savings.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.125
And then they have more cost
[INAUDIBLE] in the warehouse

00:11:19.125 --> 00:11:20.550
to run analytical queries.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.980
Now, when you bring
that into the lake, what

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.630
needs to be
happening is the lake

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:28.200
has to define these
zones or staging areas,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.180
So there are different staging
areas that have to be defined.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:35.010
So our customers end up defining
various different staging areas

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:36.600
where the data moves from.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:40.200
They bring all of raw data
from external systems,

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.120
whether it is structured,
semi-structured,

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.940
or unstructured data
into a raw stage.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:49.980
Then, they will transform it,
cleanse it, standardize it,

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:53.150
canonicalize it, and move
it into a refine stage.

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:55.720
Now, once the data
is in a refine stage,

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.420
they will run further analysis.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:58.820
They'll apply business rules.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.948
They will, again,
further cleanse the data

00:12:00.948 --> 00:12:02.490
based on the business
rules that they

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.300
have from their organization.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:06.570
And then they move it
into a curated stage,

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:09.130
then ending up into
the data warehouse.

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:11.700
So this is a very simple use
case, most common use case

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:14.790
that we see on GCP right now.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:18.840
Let's look at to the next
one, which is data lakes

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:23.050
are used from the perspective
of data science experimentation.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:28.050
So it follows a similar model,
but here, the curated data now

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:31.150
has to be accessible
to exploratory tools.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:35.610
Things like you want to
provide access to notebooks,

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.740
you want to provide
access to any

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.400
of the other machine-learning
tools that exists.

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:44.220
You want to have
access from the spout.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:48.090
All the data that is
actually in curated zone

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:51.540
has to be made accessible to all
of these different tools that

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:53.070
exist within open source.

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:57.300
And there are more that keeps
on getting added year over year.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.550
So this is the second
use case that we commonly

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:05.980
see on how people are
using data lakes on GCP.

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:08.600
The last one is an
interesting use case.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:13.050
And this is recently
becoming more popular

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:16.030
where customers
are using the data,

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:19.300
and transforming it, and
moving it into data warehouse,

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:21.100
but also they're
looking at ways where

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.290
they can take the data
from a data warehouse,

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:25.090
and put it back into the lake.

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.020
And then have a
federated way of querying

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:30.580
the data between the data
warehouse and the data lake,

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.770
have a federated
query capability

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:36.250
that allows you to bring the
data, stale data or old data,

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.620
and the new data,
combined together

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:43.900
when you are essentially
doing ad hoc analysis

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:45.460
that you are looking to do.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:51.640
All of these three use
cases, primarily what

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.370
we see with our
customers on GCP.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:58.450
Now, when you look at what goes
in building these three use

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:02.470
cases, these are the components
of GCP that makes them happen.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:05.020
Right now, we have various
different services,

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.250
and these services have
to be brought together.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:09.942
And this is something that we
will be looking in the future.

00:14:09.942 --> 00:14:11.650
But these are some of
the things that you

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:15.860
would have to bring together
in order to build your lake.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:18.970
So right now it's
do-it-yourself lake.

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:21.360
And this is going to be--

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.770
we are looking at how
we will be integrating

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.270
these various
different components

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:26.890
to make it easier for you.

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.530
But in order to
build a lake, there

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.260
are two main things that you
need from a lake perspective.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.750
First is you need
the central storage.

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:38.250
You need to have the
ability to store the data,

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.660
whether it's structured,
semi-structured,

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:44.380
or unstructured data, you need
to have a way to store that.

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:48.260
And part of GCP, there are two
services that are available.

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:50.590
One is Google Could
storage, and the other

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:53.347
is a BigQuery storage.

00:14:53.347 --> 00:14:55.180
And we look at what of
those difference are,

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:57.500
and when you should be
using one or the other.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.212
I mean, it's an
easy choice, but I

00:14:59.212 --> 00:15:01.170
would like to give you
more information on that

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:03.020
a little bit later.

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:05.090
So storage plays a vital role.

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:06.610
The second one is security.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.490
You need to be able
to have the best

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:12.550
security possible for securing
all of the data that actually

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:14.650
exists, whether on
the BigQuery site,

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:17.290
or on a cloud storage site.

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:20.860
So cloud storage provides
you the bucket-level,

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.380
or the prefix-level
access control

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:26.050
that you can define and
set on these, policies

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:27.070
that you can set.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:31.180
And BigQuery has
now added, I think,

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:34.810
we have added like column level
as well as rule level security

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:37.430
as part of BigQuery.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.733
So customers can pick
and choose how they want,

00:15:40.733 --> 00:15:42.400
and where they want
the data lake to be,

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.140
and what the data
lake storage is,

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.630
but these are the two common
choices that customers make

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:48.710
when they're picking a lake.

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:51.578
Now, when you look at how you
bring the data into the lake,

00:15:51.578 --> 00:15:53.120
there are various
different services.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:54.970
If you are bringing
data in real-time,

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:57.670
customers tend to
use [INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:00.490
And when you are looking to
move massive amounts of data

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:04.720
from your on prem
filers to GCP directly,

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.530
they end up using what we
call a transfer appliance.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.210
Transfer appliances
are you can order them.

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:12.410
They will come to
your data centers.

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:14.500
You can copy it into
it, and ship it back,

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:17.440
and then it will be
copied into the buckets

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:22.180
that you have created
for this data.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.360
Then you have data fusion
as another tool that

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:28.660
allows you to bring data, but
there are other capabilities

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:31.600
that exist within BigQuery
which are tied into BigQuery

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:33.930
from that perspective.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:35.470
[INAUDIBLE] transfer
service that

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:36.877
allows you to
bring data from all

00:16:36.877 --> 00:16:38.710
of the various different
[INAUDIBLE] sources

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:39.760
into BigQuery.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:45.790
Now the data is being ingested
in, it needs to be cataloged.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:48.070
So by default right
now, all of the data

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:50.650
that exists within
BigQuery automatically

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.770
gets registered
with data catalog.

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:57.050
But GCS, there is no
mechanism breakdown

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:01.460
by which the GCS data, the
data that gets stored on GCS,

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:03.170
gets cataloged automatically.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.849
So you would have
to end up building

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:07.730
some kind of a
metadata extractor

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:09.740
that's looking at all
of the different buckets

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:13.040
and different files that
exist within GCS buckets,

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.050
and registering them
with the catalog.

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:16.730
So that's something
that you would have

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:19.680
to build by yourself right now.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.140
So you would have to use your
favorite tool, programming

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:27.170
language of choice
to build and add

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:32.870
a crawling capability to add
metadata to the data catalog.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.090
The other thing that
is required, in order

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:37.830
to make the data
accessible, is all

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:43.670
of the data that exists within
GCS bucket, so on the BigQuery.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:48.080
So recent launched
Dataproc Metastore So this

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:50.280
is a high-compatible Metastore.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:51.890
It's based on high Metastore.

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.720
So this will allow you
to register the data,

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:57.940
structured data specifically,
so that you can query it

00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:01.220
using Spark, PRESTO, or Hive.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:03.920
So this is another
system that basically

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:05.890
allows you to bring
more of metadata

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:09.450
and organize it in a
way that is for you.

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:13.430
Now, once the data is cataloged,
and once technical metadata

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.770
has been registered,
there are various tools

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:19.340
that are available on
GCP to actually process.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:23.900
You have cloud Dataproc, which
is hosted Hadoop, managed

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:25.940
Hadoop, I would say.

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:27.350
And then you have
cloud dataflow,

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:32.080
which just provides you a server
list for a data processing

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:34.700
paradigm for code
batch, enter your time.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:36.430
And then you have
BigQuery, which

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:38.440
has the query engine
that's part of it, that

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.250
allows you to run
analytics into your time

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:44.020
on the data that's
been ingested.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.720
And then a lot of
machine learning

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.310
algorithms and techniques
that range, like, for example,

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:49.900
are automated.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:51.608
That they can be pretty
much integrated--

00:18:51.608 --> 00:18:56.500
are very much integrated
into BigQuery.

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:59.340
And there are also
approaches for connecting

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:01.110
with the data that sits on GCS.

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:04.190
Now, bringing in
all these together,

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:06.940
like when you look at it, there
are various different aspects,

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:08.740
right now, still exist.

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:10.900
That you would have to
spend a little bit more

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:12.760
time connecting them together.

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:16.210
First, you would have to
catalog all your data.

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:18.130
While the data in
BigQuery automatically

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:20.940
gets rendered in
data catalog, there

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.870
is some amount of
effort required

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:25.540
in cataloging all of the data
that exists within the cloud

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.040
storage system.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:30.160
Now, when you think
about Metastore,

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:34.530
which is a new addition
to the Cloud GCP family,

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:38.950
it provides the much-awaited
technical metadata registration

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:42.400
for searching data that allows
the data to be now exposed

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:46.200
with the most popular open
source tools, which can, again,

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:48.920
be done on Dataproc.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:50.790
So now let's look a
little bit further

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:54.300
into all of these really
just different technologies

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.920
or services that we have in GCP.

00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:58.020
Let's start with storage first.

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:02.640
So how do people use GCS?

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.152
People consider GCS
as the data lake.

00:20:05.152 --> 00:20:07.110
Some customers do that,
and then some customers

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.430
consider BigQuery
as the data lake.

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:10.230
And that's OK.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:15.010
Both do have advantages
and disadvantages,

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:18.060
but they both can be
used as the base storage

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.060
layer for a data lake.

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:24.480
Most customers end up
bringing the data into GCP,

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:27.370
using various ingestion
tools and processing systems.

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.300
It's not just limited to
whatever GCP provides.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:33.750
You could have
[INAUDIBLE] vendors

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.780
providing capabilities that
allow you to bring the data.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.310
You can use any of
the SaaS services

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:40.320
to bring the data into GCP.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:45.630
Now as you bring the data into
m the pattern that we observe

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:48.450
is, as the data goes through
various different forms

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:51.340
of processing, one of
the primary destinations

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:54.360
for this data that lands
on GCS is BigQuery.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:58.290
And that's where most of the
analytics end up happening.

00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:00.420
Because, as you guys
all know, BigQuery

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:05.620
is the data warehouse
offered by GCP.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:10.290
Now when you look at it from a
BigQuery as a data warehouse,

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:13.050
data that gets
moved into BigQuery

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:16.920
is optimized for analytics from
the beginning, from the start.

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:19.350
As soon as you import
the data into BigQuery,

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.410
it's optimized for analytics.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:27.580
Now, the way BigQuery
stores, the internal format

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:30.330
is very much
optimized in the sense

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:33.490
for doing a large-scale
analytics on data.

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:37.470
And we have customers who
stores petabytes of data,

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:41.200
and analyzed all of
that data in seconds.

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:44.460
Now, one of the differences
between BigQuery and GCS

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:48.420
is BigQuery, the data has to be
structured or semi-structured.

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:53.040
So you can store JSON and you
can store those kind of types

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:55.325
within BigQuery.

00:21:55.325 --> 00:21:57.600
But on GCS, you can store any.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:00.120
You can have images, you're
going to have audio files,

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.720
you can have pretty much
anything you want still

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.880
being stored on GCS.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.730
Now let's go a
little bit further

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:13.800
and look at what are the
differences between these.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:18.177
I want to be careful about
when we talk about cost.

00:22:18.177 --> 00:22:19.760
I think the cost,
from a cost outlook,

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:21.980
it all depends on what
you do with the BigQuery,

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:28.080
but compared to
GCS, BigQuery now

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:32.220
has optimized with fixed
logs and all those things.

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:34.380
It basically gives you
a lot more optimization

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.310
from that perspective.

00:22:36.310 --> 00:22:40.350
But the major difference that
exists between GCS and BigQuery

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:47.460
is the schema for BigQuery
has to be defined [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:49.410
The schema has to be known.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:51.420
But in GCS case, it
doesn't need to be.

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:53.670
They can actually
store just the files,

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:57.270
and they can define the
schema at that time.

00:22:57.270 --> 00:23:00.510
The common data sources for all
of the data that exists in GCS

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:03.417
is like a file
format, any data type.

00:23:03.417 --> 00:23:06.000
It doesn't matter if structured,
unstructured, semi-structured

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:07.530
data all can be stored.

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:09.690
Now, mostly BigQuery
is used from

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:12.270
a relational and
transactional databases,

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:15.390
but, as you can
imagine, there are also

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:18.120
semi-structured data that can
exist within the database.

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:19.845
For example like in Oracle.

00:23:19.845 --> 00:23:22.170
They can be Excel,
they can be JSON.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:25.410
So all of that, not
specifically Excel, but JSON

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:28.990
can be represented within
BigQuery seamlessly.

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:32.670
And the primary users,
when you look at it

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:36.490
from the perspective of these
two different services, cloud

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:40.440
storage data engineers, anyone
who can read and write a file

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:43.430
can basically access it.

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:47.010
In BigQuery, anyone who can
write a query, which are mostly

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:49.560
data analysts, business
analysts, even data

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:52.020
engineers, So pretty much
everyone on the planet

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:53.560
knows how to write a query.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:55.410
So they can use BigQuery.

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:57.660
In that sense, the data is
a little bit structured,

00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:59.035
and they would
have to understand

00:23:59.035 --> 00:24:01.440
how to write a query, which
is really common these days

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:02.070
right now.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:03.260
So anyone can do that.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.100
The only difference
being is the data

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:07.500
is pretty much
structured in BigQuery,

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:12.430
and the data is not at
all structured in GCS.

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:14.330
Now what I mean it's
structured, it's

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:17.850
the representation of the
technical method of it.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:20.010
Just like the columns,
and the types,

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:22.740
and those kind of things, not
the files and organization

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:25.670
of those files.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:29.680
So these GCS and
BigQuery stories

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:33.030
becomes the foundation for any
data lake that you're building.

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:34.640
So depending on
your use cases, you

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:36.020
would have to make a choice.

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:38.350
Do you want to start with GCS?

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:40.475
Or do you want directly
land the data into BigQuery

00:24:40.475 --> 00:24:42.930
and that becomes your
data lake storage?

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:47.130
Now, when you think
about it, in a few cases,

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:50.630
customers still want to
land the data in GCS,

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.910
and progressive to
GCS, and then load

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:55.730
only the data that isn't
required and curated

00:24:55.730 --> 00:25:00.490
for analytics into BigQuery
for business analysis.

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:02.760
Now that we have looked
at storage options,

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:05.920
let's look at the catalog
and the metadata options.

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:10.690
The catalog in the metadata
options is the Metastore.

00:25:10.690 --> 00:25:14.190
Now, as you guys all
know, data catalog

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:18.720
provides a central
catalog across GCP

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:21.660
for sorting all of the
metadata, indexing the metadata,

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:23.850
and making it much more
accessible to a search

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:25.010
interface,

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:27.900
So the interesting
part of all of this

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:31.410
is it also adds on
ability to create

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:35.050
some of the data governance
capabilities integrated

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:36.220
as part of that.

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:37.950
So you can use data
governance templates

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:41.010
to categorize various different
data assets within the catalog.

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:45.780
So right now, this is
very deeply integrated

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:49.580
from a BigQuery perspective,
but there is, in the future,

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:52.080
that there might be integration
automatically being provided

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:55.210
from a GCS perspective.

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:56.730
But in the meantime,
you would have

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.600
to have a system
that can analyze,

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:02.010
pick all of the metadata
that exists on GCS,

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:05.970
and populate that
into the data catalog.

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:09.190
So after tagging PII data.

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:12.510
So this is basically using
DLP, Data Loss Prevention--

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:15.060
it's yet another
service of GCP--

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:16.950
that allows you to
analyze the data

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.110
and identify any of
the sensitive data that

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:20.850
exists within that.

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:23.490
If there is any PII data,
and a lot of time that

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:28.780
PII data and surveys to the
administrators to act on

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:31.000
it once identified.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.720
The data catalog also
provides the ability

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:36.600
where you have the
ability to control who

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.790
has access to what metadata.

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.100
Some of the parts--

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:44.180
as the data is important, the
metadata associated with that

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:45.610
also is very important.

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:48.570
So having ability to
provide how to have

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:52.140
access control over metadata
is a critical capability

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.050
that data catalog provides.

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:56.460
Now this is for all of
the metadata that exists.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:58.380
And, as you all know,
there are three kinds up

00:26:58.380 --> 00:26:59.640
metadata that exists--

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:02.010
technical, business and,
operational metadata.

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:04.500
So we're talking about
a business metadata,

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:07.560
and this is also incorporating
some of the technical metadata.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:10.920
But this is not from the
viewpoint of processing.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:14.490
This is just for making
the data accessible to you,

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:17.380
and making it easier to access.

00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:19.500
Now, the next one
is more focusing

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:24.330
on how do you take technical
metadata and [INAUDIBLE] that

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:26.700
with the Metastore,
that then that allows

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:29.630
you to make it accessible.

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:31.940
Then that data becomes
accessible to open

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:35.020
source tools that exist
within the ecosystem.

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:39.980
So Dataproc Metastores is
a newly-launched service.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:43.880
It's a fully-managed
highly-available open source

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:47.450
native, Apache Hive metastore
service which simplifies

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:50.150
technical metadata management.

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:53.110
All of the technical
metadata that exists

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:56.630
and that gets registered
within the Dataproc Metastore

00:27:56.630 --> 00:28:02.480
is now accessible to Spark,
Hive, PRESTO, Notebooks.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:05.980
All of these tools that
are popular in open source

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:11.280
already have ready access to the
data that exists in the lake.

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:12.860
Now, it's an alpha feature.

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:16.700
You should please go take it
for a spin, and give it a try.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:19.160
And let us know if there
are things that you would

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:21.540
like to see improved on it.

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:25.340
But it's a great addition
to telling the true story

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:28.730
of a data lake on GCP.

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:32.240
Now, what does the
Dataproc Metastore provide?

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:34.250
It provides a
simplified management.

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:37.820
It allows you to create and
configure Dataproc Metastore.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.880
It even comes out with
kerberos clusters,

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:42.980
so you can configure
a [? KDC ?] and do

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:44.570
all of those kind of stuff.

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:47.170
It provides the
[INAUDIBLE] capability.

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:50.970
And it's all integrated into
the standard console of GCP,

00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:53.750
so you get a
fully-managed experience

00:28:53.750 --> 00:28:58.960
for managing one or more
Metastores in your lake.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:05.183
Now that we have talked about
the storage and the metadata,

00:29:05.183 --> 00:29:06.850
we can look at the
other components that

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:12.220
exist within the lake,
critical components that

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:13.780
exist within the lake.

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:17.480
Now, data exploration analysis
is an important aspect of it.

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:20.590
Now, because we talked about
registering the metadata,

00:29:20.590 --> 00:29:24.190
and with Dataproc Metastore,
you should be able to,

00:29:24.190 --> 00:29:25.570
as soon as you
bring the data in,

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:28.180
you want to do some kind
of an exploratory analysis.

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:34.300
You should be able to use tools
like Hive to preview the data.

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:37.210
Now the data, because it
is registered with Hive,

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:43.600
you'll be able to query the
data using a Hive or Spark right

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:46.010
from the console.

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:49.510
Now, because of the launch
of Dataproc notebooks,

00:29:49.510 --> 00:29:51.670
the notebooks are also
integrated with all

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:55.460
of this data, making exploratory
analysis much more easy

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:56.800
and fun.

00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:58.820
So you don't have to
have a separate system.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:00.970
You don't have to manage
it all by yourself.

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:04.120
It's all managed
within the Dataproc,

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:06.160
and makes it extremely
easy to get access

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:09.140
to all of the data that gets
registered with the Dataproc

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:09.970
Metastore.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:12.460
Now, there is a little bit
of a manual process where

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:16.390
you would have to register for
the data that already exists

00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:19.090
on GCS, and not register
with the Hive Metastore.

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:21.220
You would have to
do that manually,

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:23.720
like I have alluded
to that before.

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:26.210
But that's something
that, in the future,

00:30:26.210 --> 00:30:29.110
we might basically
make it automatic.

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:32.440
But for right now, you will have
to write something that'll just

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:35.430
pull all of that stuff
and put it in metadata,

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:36.760
and put it in there.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:39.770
Now what are the use
cases that I talked about?

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:43.320
So you want to have ability
to spread the data between,

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:45.555
for example, between
BigQuery and GCS.

00:30:45.555 --> 00:30:47.680
Now, the interesting thing
is how do you figure out

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:48.850
what is between that?

00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:52.120
So BigQuery has recently
launched the ability

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:55.000
to query the data
using federated tables.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.920
So you can define that
as an external table

00:30:56.920 --> 00:30:59.170
for data that's sitting on GCS.

00:30:59.170 --> 00:31:01.360
And partly, part of the
data could be in BigQuery.

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:02.050
You can join.

00:31:02.050 --> 00:31:04.990
You can do all normal operations
that you can do with the table,

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:07.970
can be done with
federated queries.

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:10.780
So here, it's a simple
process of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.330
Now wouldn't it be cool
if all of the data that

00:31:13.330 --> 00:31:16.930
sits on GCS and any other
system automatically

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:20.170
gets registered into
Hive and gets represented

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:22.060
as an external
table in BigQuery,

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:25.300
so that any tool that
exists in the ecosystem

00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:30.100
can use the metadata
that is being registered

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:32.830
to query the data, and make
it immediately accessible.

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:37.600
So the data lake doesn't have
to end up being a silo storage,

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:41.530
but making it much more
accessible to these ecosystem

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:44.130
tools that are
basically providing

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:46.870
that additional service to
form a registration of metadata

00:31:46.870 --> 00:31:48.670
perspective.

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:52.020
So transformations.

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:54.960
We have talked about when
you build a lake, yes, you

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:57.270
want to have the ability
to transform your data.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:01.290
You want the ability to do
complex transformations on it.

00:32:01.290 --> 00:32:04.920
But we have never seen--

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:09.500
at least I have never
seen a simple job

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:12.200
that's running within the lake.

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:14.600
It's a complex
network of hundreds

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:16.470
of jobs that are
actually running

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:19.250
in the lake in many enterprises.

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:23.147
And in order to control all of
that, and manage all of that,

00:32:23.147 --> 00:32:23.980
you need two things.

00:32:23.980 --> 00:32:27.478
One, first of all, you need
the ability to run the job

00:32:27.478 --> 00:32:28.520
to do the transformation.

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:33.350
So you can use Dataproc
or you can use Dataflow

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:34.580
to do that [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:38.610
So Dataflow is a [INAUDIBLE]
offering for [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:40.490
earlier time stream processing.

00:32:40.490 --> 00:32:44.180
And Dataproc is basically
supporting both Hadoop

00:32:44.180 --> 00:32:46.010
and Spark [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:47.820
Now, what do we
need to do for this

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:52.190
is to be able to take these two
things together and orchestrate

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:53.870
them using Cloud Composer.

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:56.820
So while I can
essentially create jobs,

00:32:56.820 --> 00:33:01.170
I want to be able to stitch
them together in a bag,

00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:03.530
and that can be
done with Composer.

00:33:03.530 --> 00:33:07.040
In addition to this, you can,
of course, look at Data Fusion

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:08.750
as a way of doing
transformations.

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:11.300
But Data Fusion
underneath uses Dataproc

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:13.340
and provides a visual tool.

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:14.930
It's a data innovation tool.

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:18.395
It provides an ability to do
the similar stuff on top of it,

00:33:18.395 --> 00:33:20.270
where you don't need to
have any programming.

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:23.300
You can do just do it
in a code-free manner,

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:25.775
So now that we have looked
at all of these things--

00:33:25.775 --> 00:33:30.360
the storage, the metadata,
the transformation--

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:32.330
now the last thing
that is required

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:36.980
is the ability to bring
all of the data integration

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:39.680
aspects and the ability to look
at the lineage edge-to-end.

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:43.110
Because when you look at how the
data flows within data lake--

00:33:45.630 --> 00:33:48.960
so while Dataflow and
Dataproc provides the advanced

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:51.000
transformation capabilities,
data integration

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:55.260
like Data Fusion provides much
more a manage-detailed solution

00:33:55.260 --> 00:34:00.120
with no-code retail pipeline,

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:02.730
So you need to also
have ingestion template,

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:04.980
and ingestion template
for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:10.590
So you should be able to look
at as an example of BTU transfer

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:14.219
service, it provides
a very simple form

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:15.690
that allows you to look at--

00:34:18.940 --> 00:34:20.820
select a source,
select a destination,

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:24.540
be able to just copy the
data over into the target.

00:34:24.540 --> 00:34:27.780
So you need to have the
ability to do simple ingestion.

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:31.770
And the data lake should not
limit only for the usage of--

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:34.320
you should be able to
use any form of Google.

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:36.739
So we have customers
who use Informatica,

00:34:36.739 --> 00:34:37.920
we have people use Talent.

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:40.760
People use [INAUDIBLE]
to bring the data in.

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:43.690
And then they basically ETL it.

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:45.270
Here you can use,
as an option, you

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:46.900
can use Data Fusion to do that.

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:49.469
You can you also use
Data Fusion for doing

00:34:49.469 --> 00:34:51.420
additional transformations,
if you like,

00:34:51.420 --> 00:34:53.190
and deploy them into the lake.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:56.639
All the data that gets
generated, gets deployed back

00:34:56.639 --> 00:35:00.330
into the lake, or it can be
pushed into the data warehouse.

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:03.660
Now, one of the important
things from a large enterprise

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:06.060
perspective is ability
to keep track of the data

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:07.690
as it flows to the system.

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:11.340
So you need to have a good
understanding of how the data

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:13.680
is flowing in your lake.

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:16.620
So you need to have
good lineage capability.

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:19.530
While this lineage capability
right now exists only

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:21.780
in Data Fusion,
in the future, we

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.510
will be looking at making that
available as part of the data

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:25.500
catalog.

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:28.050
And once it's there,
you will be able to look

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:31.170
at lineage across the
board, end-to-end,

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:33.840
from that perspective,
from a GCP perspective,

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:36.080
for any data that exists.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:38.220
Now the only limitation
of the current lineage

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:41.250
is that if you use Data
Fusion, only then that lineage

00:35:41.250 --> 00:35:42.600
will be present.

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:44.460
The lineage is
available at two levels.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:47.010
One is at a data set
level, which tells you

00:35:47.010 --> 00:35:49.860
what job process worked
on what data set,

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:52.950
and what are the resulting
data sets that were generated.

00:35:52.950 --> 00:35:55.730
The second is be able to
look at the field level.

00:35:55.730 --> 00:35:59.190
The field level will give you a
much more granular information

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:01.063
of how an individual
field was generated,

00:36:01.063 --> 00:36:02.730
and what the various
different data sets

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:04.520
that were connected to it--

00:36:04.520 --> 00:36:10.020
the template, the resulting
theme from those operations.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:15.840
So these will provide from
a compliance or the ability.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:18.120
It provides an
end-to-end solution

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:22.350
to have the best
possible data lake,

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:25.680
So now that we have looked
at various different aspects

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:26.465
of data lake--

00:36:26.465 --> 00:36:28.590
so we have looked at what
are the different storage

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:35.670
mechanisms we have in the lake,
what are the different metadata

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:38.850
systems they have, what
are the various different

00:36:38.850 --> 00:36:42.150
transformation systems
we have, and what do you

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:44.740
do from a data integration
business perspective.

00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:48.540
So bringing it all together
right now is a task.

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:51.650
It's something that you
would have to do yourself.

00:36:51.650 --> 00:36:54.870
But in the future,
we are providing

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:57.990
all these deeper integrations
with various services

00:36:57.990 --> 00:37:01.620
to make the life of building
data lakes much more easier.

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:04.860
It should be very easy
for you to just take

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:09.780
all of these integrated set
of services, and build lakes.

00:37:09.780 --> 00:37:12.910
Our goal is to make
it as fast as you can.

00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:15.390
Right now it probably
takes weeks or months,

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:18.500
so we should be able to
reduce that down to--

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:20.910
with all these tool integrations
that end up happening,

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:23.820
it should come
down to a few days.

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:28.290
Now, to summarize, as we
found during the talk,

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:32.150
building data lakes
is extremely hard.

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:34.980
Now, GCP provides all of
the necessary services

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:39.180
to build the best data
lakes you can possibly have.

00:37:39.180 --> 00:37:41.430
As you can also see,
all of the innovation

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:44.580
that is happening
within GCP to build

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:45.840
the best-integrated parts.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:48.150
As an example,
BigQuery now integrates

00:37:48.150 --> 00:37:51.230
with data that exists on GCS,
allowing you to federate data

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:53.340
across BigQuery and GCS.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:56.820
In addition to completing
the story for data lake,

00:37:56.820 --> 00:37:59.540
we are closing a lot
of different gaps.

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:02.180
Dataproc Metastore is
another SaaS service

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:05.150
that just got launched that
provides you the ability

00:38:05.150 --> 00:38:09.110
to integrate metadata and make
it accessible more open source

00:38:09.110 --> 00:38:10.460
tools.

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:13.220
And these are some of
the capability that

00:38:13.220 --> 00:38:17.380
exists in GCP that you can
leverage to build the best

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:18.860
lake possible.

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:20.490
Give it a try.

00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:22.360
Thank you so much.

